3.3  ECC and failing RAM

TL;DR
  - RAM can return a wrong bit, mostly from a failing or badly seated module.
    Without ECC nothing notices: random crashes, corrupted files.
  - ECC memory stores extra check bits: it corrects a single wrong bit and
    detects two, and the kernel counts both (EDAC).
  - Corrected errors are a warning; a count that keeps growing on one module
    means replacing it. An uncorrectable error stops the program, or the
    machine.

      sudo ras-mc-ctl --error-count       # per module (package rasdaemon)
      journalctl -k | grep -i -E 'edac|mce|hardware error'

On your machine
  ECC       no memory controller reports errors (EDAC): RAM without ECC, no
            driver, or a virtual machine
  Retired   none: the kernel retired no RAM after an error

Pitfalls
  - Random segfaults in different programs, or a compiler crashing on code that
    built yesterday, point at RAM: test it before reinstalling.
  - Test with memtest86+ from a USB stick or a boot menu entry, for several
    passes. Testing from the running system can't reach every address.
  - ECC needs a CPU, board and modules that all support it: ECC modules in a
    desktop board often run without it.
  - No EDAC entries doesn't prove good RAM: it may only mean there's no ECC.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does ECC correct a bit?

    For every 64 bits of data, an ECC module stores 8 more: a code computed from
    the data (SECDED: single error correct, double error detect). On each read,
    the controller recomputes it. A single flipped bit gives a pattern that
    points at the bit, which is fixed on the fly; two flipped bits are detected
    but can't be located.

  ▾ What happens on an uncorrectable error?

    The CPU raises a machine check. If the bad memory belongs to a process, the
    kernel can kill just that process and retire the page, so it's never used
    again (HardwareCorrupted in /proc/meminfo); if the kernel itself was using
    it, the machine panics rather than carry on with wrong data.

Related
  Commands  ras-mc-ctl, journalctl
  Paths     /sys/devices/system/edac/mc/
  Hub       idk memory Memory
  See also  idk 3.1 RAM and the memory hierarchy
  See also  idk 21.3 dmesg and the kernel log
